    "title": "Sean Penn wins Supporting Actor Oscar, gets shade from Kieran Culkin",
    "text": "One of the most topsy-turvy Oscar categories of the year has finally been declared. Sean Penn won Best Supporting Actor on Sunday night for his heralded role in One Battle After Another. But he didn't appear at the ceremony to accept his trophy, the third of his career. In his absence, presenter Kieran Culkin ribbed him at the podium. \"Sean Penn couldn't be here this evening — or didn't want to — so I'll be accepting the award on his behalf,\" last year's Best Supporting Actor winner for A Real Pain declared. Penn also didn't show up at the BAFTA Awards or Actor Awards, where he won at both. The New York Times reported that Penn was en route to Ukraine, and on Monday, President Volodymyr Zelenskyy posted on X a photo of his meeting with the actor. \"Sean, thanks to you, we know what a true friend of Ukraine is,\" he wrote in his post. \"You have stood with Ukraine since the first day of the full-scale war. This is still true today. And we know that you will continue to stand with our country and our people.\" At the Oscars, Penn took down co-nominees Benicio Del Toro (One Battle After Another), Jacob Elordi (Frankenstein), Delroy Lindo (Sinners), and Stellan Skarsgård (Sentimental Value). \"The show just started and we already have a no-show,\" Oscars host Conan O'Brien joked after a commercial break. \"This thing's a humdinger — you gotta stay tuned!\" The Gold Derby data predicted that Penn would prevail following his victories at BAFTA and Actor Awards. Elsewhere this awards season, Elordi prevailed at Critics Choice, Skarsgård at Golden Globes, Del Toro at National Board of Review, and Lindo at NAACP Image Awards. Phew! No wonder this was one of the year's hardest categories to predict. This marks Penn's third Oscar win after Mystic River (lead, 2003) and Milk (lead, 2008); he was also nominated for Dead Man Walking (lead, 1995), Sweet and Lowdown (lead, 1999), and I Am Sam (lead, 2001). He plays Col. Steven J. Lockjaw in Paul Thomas Anderson's One Battle After Another, the antagonist who kidnaps Leonardo DiCaprio's character's daughter. Penn makes history by becoming only the fourth male actor to win three acting Oscars, joining Daniel Day-Lewis, Jack Nicholson, and Walter Brennan. The four women to accomplish that feat are Katharine Hepburn (the only person with four acting Oscars), Ingrid Bergman, Meryl Streep, and Frances McDormand (who has a fourth for producing). See the full winners list for the 2026 Oscars. Oscar’s 3-Timers Club: The acting winners who’ve accomplished the rare trifecta Updated 3/16 with X post.",
